the parent is missing, when the name is taken by a file,\0A/// or when permission is refused. A directory that already exists is\0A/// also an `Err`, so a caller who does not care should check\0A/// `Os.exists` first.\0Apub let make_dir (path: String): Result[Unit, String] =\0A    if os_make_dir_raw(path) == 0 { Ok(()) }\0A    else { Err(\22Os.make_dir: could not create \22.concat(path)) }\0A\0A/// Atomic within one filesystem, and an error rather than a silent\0A/// copy across two. The point is that the destination is never a\0A/// half-written file.\0Apub let rename_file (from: String) (to: String): Result[Unit, String] =\0A    if os_rename_file_raw(from, to) == 0 { Ok(()) }\0A    else { Err(\22Os.rename_file: could not rename \22.concat(from).concat(\22 to \22).concat(to)) }\0A\0A/// Deletes a file. `Err` when it is not there, when it is a directory,\0A/// or when permission is refused — `Os.remove_tree` is the one for\0A/// directories.\0Apub let remove_file (path: String): Result[Unit, String] =\0A    if os_remove_file_raw(path) == 0 { Ok(()) }\0A    else { Err(\22Os.remove_file: could not remove \22.concat(path)) }\0A\0A/// Deletes a file or directory tree. A final symlink is removed even\0A/// with trailing separators; links encountered in the tree are never\0A/// followed. Ancestors of the requested path resolve normally when the\0A/// starting directory is acquired; traversal then uses held directories.\0A/// Concurrent changes or resource exhaustion can return `Err` after partial\0A/// removal. Traversal is limited to 128 directory levels to bound native\0A/// stack, enumeration storage, and open handles. Empty paths and final\0A/// `.` or `..` components are rejected.\0Apub let remove_tree (path: String): Result[Unit, String] =\0A    if os_remove_tree_raw(path) == 0 { Ok(()) }\0A    else { Err(\22Os.remove_tree: could not remove \22.concat(path)) }\0A\0A/// Copies the CONTENTS of `src` into `dst`, which must already exist\0A/// and must not overlap `src`